# What must never end up inside the image.
#
# There was no .dockerignore here, and the cost was not subtle: `COPY . .`
# produced an 8.53GB layer against 271MB of installed dependencies, because
# data/ holds the downloaded source dumps — a 6.9GB AO3 metadata JSONL and a
# 1.1GB HuggingFace cache. Every backend image carried a private copy of both.
#
# That is paid three times over. The image is ~12GB instead of ~800MB; every
# build ships an 8GB context to the daemon before it can start; and every
# retained deploy generation costs another copy on a disk that runs at 83%.
#
# data/ is not lost by excluding it. In dev the repo is bind-mounted, so it is
# there at /app/data regardless. In the public tier the worker mounts a named
# volume at the same path. Nothing reads these files at import time — they are
# inputs to importer scripts run by hand — so an image without them is fully
# functional and can re-download on demand.
data/

# Python build artefacts. Stale .pyc files compiled against a different
# interpreter are worse than useless in an image.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Secrets. The image is built from this directory; a stray .env would be baked
# in and readable by anyone who can pull or run it.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Local noise.
*.log
*.dump
*.sqlite
.venv/
venv/
.DS_Store

# tests/ is deliberately NOT excluded: the documented way to run the suite is
# `docker exec ficatlas-backend-1 python -m pytest tests/`, which needs them
# present in the image.
